# Keep the ETL service's build context small -- this image only needs to run
# the Python pipeline, not the frontend.
.git/

# ── data/ ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Until 2026-11-05 all of data/ was excluded, because /app/data was a mounted
# Railway volume holding db.sqlite. There is no volume now and no SQLite: the
# corpus is in Postgres. What remains under data/ that git tracks is committed
# INPUT the cron reads -- data/guard_registry.json above all, which
# ETL/lib/guard_verdicts.py needs to run any guard, and which the old blanket
# exclusion kept out of every image (its `!data/guard_registry.json` was inert,
# see git history). So data/ is now carried, minus everything a pipeline run
# GENERATES there, which must never be baked into an image.
data/*.sqlite
data/*.sqlite-*
data/raw/
data/processed/
data/backups/
data/ranker_cache/

__pycache__/
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# Secrets must never be baked into an image. `COPY . .` would otherwise carry
# .env.local into every layer of a registry image, and the file holds the
# Anthropic, OpenAI, Voyage and Postgres credentials. Railway injects the real
# environment at runtime; nothing in the image should need this file.
.env
.env.*
# Worktrees carry a `.git` FILE rather than a directory, which the `.git/`
# pattern above does not match, and a dangling symlink inside one fails the
# build with an unhelpful COPY error.
.git
.claude/

# A database backup must no more enter an image than a credential does. This
# directory is 123 MB of verified Postgres dumps (ETL/lib/backup_postgres.py),
# untracked and, until now, matched by nothing here -- so `COPY . .` would have
# baked it into every layer, more than doubling a 77 MB build context and
# putting a full copy of the corpus in a registry. Railway mounts the real data
# on a volume; nothing in this image should ever read a backup.
postgres-backup/

# corpus-ledger/ is the committed text copy of the authored instrument corpus.
# The ETL image never reads it (restore is a checkout operation), and it grows
# with every export -- 11 MB today. Keep it out of the build context.
corpus-ledger/
